# Pre-freeze verification attempts

Both earlier attempts are preserved. Neither produced an accepted result, and
the reason in each case is recorded rather than tidied away.

## attempt_1_write_confined

Ran with its working root set to `verification_codex_pre_freeze/` and writes
confined to that directory. It completed and returned
`VERIFICATION_FAIL_PRE_FREEZE_R7`.

Its failures were not findings about the package. Items 7, 8 and 9 require
running scripts and test suites that write into `build/`, `work/` and
`verification/selftest_runtime/`; the sandbox denied those writes, so the
suites errored (160 errors in the controller suite, 60 in the package suite)
and the scripts could not run at all. Items 4, 5 and 10 failed on its own
harness rather than on the package: item 4 counted top-level keys instead of
walking `progress["audits"]` and reported 0 audits and 0 phases; item 5
matched on phrases and reported `max_attempts_3: false` for a file that
assigns `MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_PHASE = 3`; item 10 compared file digests where the
same certificate is stored as PEM on one side and DER on the other.

Items 1, 2, 3 and 11 passed and are the parts of that run that measured
something: R4 1915/0/0, R5 14159/0/1 with the single known `MODE` mismatch,
R6 14781/0/0, and the seven preserved R6 stderr lines quoted verbatim.

## attempt_2_interrupted_concurrent

Started with the four required write areas granted and a prompt corrected on
the three points above. It was **interrupted deliberately and did not finish**.

While it was running, attempt 1's process was still alive. Two Codex processes
were writing into the same directory at once, which is the single-writer
condition this orchestration requires and which was violated by launching the
second before confirming the first had exited. The check that missed it
matched the string `codex` against a process list that also contained the
supervising session's own command line, so it reported no Codex running while
two were — a tool answering a different question than the one asked.

Both processes were stopped. A snapshot taken before and after showed what
they had touched: their own directory, `build/R7_STATIC_SAFETY_REPORT.json`,
`build/R7_CHANGED_FILES.sha256` and `work/_rehearsal_r7/REHEARSAL_REPORT.json`
— all inside the permitted mutable areas — and nothing under R4, R5, R6,
`automation/`, `references/`, `corpora/`, `tools/`, `lineage/` or `state/`.

